iple date formats\n6. UTF-8 encoding\n7. Testing: BDD GWT structured PyTest\n\nThe prompts.txt mentions \"test using BDD GWT structured PyTest\" - this is a hint about the test format expected. GWT = Given/When/Then.\n\nGiven the venv has pytest, I'll:\n1. Implement the MCP server using the official `mcp` Python SDK if I can install it, OR implement a minimal MCP-compatible server using stdio.\n2. Write a data access layer that loads and normalizes the CSV data.\n3. Write query functions for each capability.\n4. Write BDD GWT-style pytest tests.\n\nLet me try installing the mcp package. Actually, since the environment is offline-ish, let me try. But the safer approach given the bare venv is to implement a minimal MCP server myself using stdio JSON-RPC. This is more reliable and doesn't depend on network.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The benchmark wants the code to \"build and tests pass\". The most robust approach: implement a clean Python package with:\n- `data_loader.py` - loads and normalizes all CSVs\n- `queries.py` - query functions (matches, teams, players, competitions, stats)\n- `mcp_server.py` - MCP server exposing tools via JSON-RPC over stdio\n- `tests/test_*.py` - BDD GWT pytest tests\n\nI'll implement a minimal but spec-compliant MCP server (JSON-RPC 2.0 over stdio with initialize, tools/list, tools/call). This avoids external dependencies. Let me make it dependency-light (only stdlib + pandas? no, pandas isn't installed). I'll use the csv module from stdlib.\n\nLet me check if I can install packages.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_058f15146001nldKok5vV2gFUF","sessionID":"ses_fa710145bffe992PbNwQxTi5bS","messageID":"msg_058f11999001bmCUGfpWzn5xfK"}}
