{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788198916033,"sessionID":"ses_fa710145bffe992PbNwQxTi5bS","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"01a058f5c8e05d21aad44309cecbcefd","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-e6k_mc30/retort-b02a3ecfac7e/brazilian_soccer_mcp/data_loader.py","oldString":"_STATE_SUFFIX_RE = re.compile(r\"\\s*[-\\u2013]\\s*[A-Z]{2}$\")\n_TRAILING_STATE_RE = re.compile(r\"\\s*[-\\u2013]\\s*[A-Z]{2}\\b\")\n\n\ndef _strip_accents(text: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return *text* with combining accents removed (NFKD decomposition).\"\"\"\n    nfkd = unicodedata.normalize(\"NFKD\", text)\n    return \"\".join(ch for ch in nfkd if not unicodedata.combining(ch))\n\n\ndef normalize_team_name(name: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return a canonical lowercase alphanumeric key for a team name.\n\n    Examples\n    --------\n    >>> normalize_team_name(\"Palmeiras-SP\")\n    'palmeiras'\n    >>> normalize_team_name(\"América - MG\")\n    'america'\n    >>> normalize_team_name(\"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\")\n    'sportclubcorinthianspaulista'\n    >>> normalize_team_name(\"Atlético-MG\")\n    'atletico'\n    \"\"\"\n    if name is None:\n        return \"\"\n    s = str(name).strip()\n    # Remove a trailing \"-XX\" / \" - XX\" two-letter state suffix (case-sensitive\n    # on the original string so we don't eat legitimate trailing capitals).\n    s = _TRAILING_STATE_RE.sub(\"\", s)\n    s = _strip_accents(s)\n    s = s.lower()\n    s = re.sub(r\"[^a-z0-9]\", \"\", s)\n    return s\n\n\ndef display_team_name(name: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return a human-friendly team name with the state suffix removed.\"\"\"\n    if name is None:\n        return \"\"\n    s = str(name).strip()\n    s = _STATE_SUFFIX_RE.sub(\"\", s)\n    # also handle \" - XX\" mid/ trailing\n    s = re.sub(r\"\\s*[-\\u2013]\\s*[A-Z]{2}\\b\", \"\", s)\n    return s.strip()","newString":"# Matches a trailing two-letter Brazilian state suffix, e.g. \"-SP\", \" - MG\".\n_STATE_SUFFIX_RE = re.compile(r\"\\s*[-\\u2013]\\s*([A-Z]{2})\\s*$\")\n\n\ndef _strip_accents(text: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return *text* with combining accents removed (NFKD decomposition).\"\"\"\n    nfkd = unicodedata.normalize(\"NFKD\", text)\n    return \"\".join(ch for ch in nfkd if not unicodedata.combining(ch))\n\n\ndef _bare_key(name: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Lowercase alphanumeric key with the state suffix removed.\"\"\"\n    if name is None:\n        return \"\"\n    s = str(name).strip()\n    s = _STATE_SUFFIX_RE.sub(\"\", s)\n    s = _strip_accents(s)\n    s = s.lower()\n    s = re.sub(r\"[^a-z0-9]\", \"\", s)\n    return s\n\n\ndef extract_state(name: str, fallback_state: str = \"\") -> str:\n    \"\"\"Extract a two-letter state suffix from a team name, else return fallback.\"\"\"\n    if name:\n        m = _STATE_SUFFIX_RE.search(str(name).strip())\n        if m:\n            return m.group(1)\n    return (fallback_state or \"\").strip().upper()\n\n\ndef normalize_team_name(name: str, state: str = \"\") -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return a canonical lowercase key for a team name.\n\n    When *state* is provided (or embedded in *name* as a ``-XX`` suffix) it is\n    appended to the key so that distinct same-name clubs from different states\n    (e.g. Atlético-MG vs Atletico-PR) are not merged. When the bare name is\n    globally unique the state is redundant and the bare key alone is returned\n    by the :class:`TeamResolver`; this function is the low-level primitive.\n\n    Examples\n    --------\n    >>> normalize_team_name(\"Palmeiras-SP\")\n    'palmeiras|SP'\n    >>> normalize_team_name(\"Palmeiras\")\n    'palmeiras'\n    >>> normalize_team_name(\"Atlético-MG\")\n    'atletico|MG'\n    \"\"\"\n    if name is None:\n        return \"\"\n    bare = _bare_key(name)\n    st = extract_state(name, state)\n    if st:\n        return f\"{bare}|{st}\"\n    return bare\n\n\ndef display_team_name(name: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return a human-friendly team name with the state suffix removed.\"\"\"\n    if name is None:\n        return \"\"\n    s = str(name).strip()\n    s = _STATE_SUFFIX_RE.sub(\"\", s)\n    return s.strip()\n\n\nclass TeamResolver:\n    \"\"\"Resolve raw team names to canonical keys.\n\n    Two-phase strategy:\n    1. Observe every (bare_key, state) pair seen across the datasets.\n    2. A bare_key that maps to more than one state is *ambiguous* and the\n       state becomes part of the canonical key (``bare|SP``). A bare_key seen\n       with at most one state resolves to the bare_key alone, so\n       ``Palmeiras-SP`` and ``Palmeiras`` collapse to ``palmeiras``.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self._bare_states: dict[str, set[str]] = {}\n        self._locked = False\n\n    def observe(self, bare_key: str, state: str) -> None:\n        if self._locked:\n            return\n        st = (state or \"\").strip().upper()\n        if not bare_key:\n            return\n        self._bare_states.setdefault(bare_key, set())\n        if st:\n            self._bare_states[bare_key].add(st)\n\n    def lock(self) -> None:\n        self._locked = True\n\n    def resolve(self, bare_key: str, state: str = \"\") -> str:\n        \"\"\"Return the canonical key for an observed (bare, state) pair.\"\"\"\n        if not bare_key:\n            return \"\"\n        st = (state or \"\").strip().upper()\n        states = self._bare_states.get(bare_key, set())\n        # Ambiguous bare name: state is required to disambiguate.\n        if len(states) > 1:\n            return f\"{bare_key}|{st}\" if st else bare_key\n        # Unambiguous: collapse to bare key (state is redundant).\n        return bare_key"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-e6k_mc30/retort-b02a3ecfac7e/brazilian_soccer_mcp/data_loader.py\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-e6k_mc30/retort-b02a3ecfac7e/brazilian_soccer_mcp/data_loader.py\n+++ /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-e6k_mc30/retort-b02a3ecfac7e/brazilian_soccer_mcp/data_loader.py\n@@ -42,55 +42,116 @@\n # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n # Canonical team-name normalization\n # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n \n-_STATE_SUFFIX_RE = re.compile(r\"\\s*[-\\u2013]\\s*[A-Z]{2}$\")\n-_TRAILING_STATE_RE = re.compile(r\"\\s*[-\\u2013]\\s*[A-Z]{2}\\b\")\n+# Matches a trailing two-letter Brazilian state suffix, e.g. \"-SP\", \" - MG\".\n+_STATE_SUFFIX_RE = re.compile(r\"\\s*[-\\u2013]\\s*([A-Z]{2})\\s*$\")\n \n \n def _strip_accents(text: str) -> str:\n     \"\"\"Return *text* with combining accents removed (NFKD decomposition).\"\"\"\n     nfkd = unicodedata.normalize(\"NFKD\", text)\n     return \"\".join(ch for ch in nfkd if not unicodedata.combining(ch))\n \n \n-def normalize_team_name(name: str) -> str:\n-    \"\"\"Return a canonical lowercase alphanumeric key for a team name.\n+def _bare_key(name: str) -> str:\n+    \"\"\"Lowercase alphanumeric key with the state suffix removed.\"\"\"\n+    if name is None:\n+        return \"\"\n+    s = str(name).strip()\n+    s = _STATE_SUFFIX_RE.sub(\"\", s)\n+    s = _strip_accents(s)\n+    s = s.lower()\n+    s = re.sub(r\"[^a-z0-9]\", \"\", s)\n+    return s\n \n+\n+def extract_state(name: str, fallback_state: str = \"\") -> str:\n+    \"\"\"Extract a two-letter state suffix from a team name, else return fallback.\"\"\"\n+    if name:\n+        m = _STATE_SUFFIX_RE.search(str(name).strip())\n+        if m:\n+            return m.group(1)\n+    return (fallback_state or \"\").strip().upper()\n+\n+\n+def normalize_team_name(name: str, state: str = \"\") -> str:\n+    \"\"\"Return a canonical lowercase key for a team name.\n+\n+    When *state* is provided (or embedded in *name* as a ``-XX`` suffix) it is\n+    appended to the key so that distinct same-name clubs from different states\n+    (e.g. Atlético-MG vs Atletico-PR) are not merged. When the bare name is\n+    globally unique the state is redundant and the bare key alone is returned\n+    by the :class:`TeamResolver`; this function is the low-level primitive.\n+\n     Examples\n     --------\n     >>> normalize_team_name(\"Palmeiras-SP\")\n+    'palmeiras|SP'\n+    >>> normalize_team_name(\"Palmeiras\")\n     'palmeiras'\n-    >>> normalize_team_name(\"América - MG\")\n-    'america'\n-    >>> normalize_team_name(\"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\")\n-    'sportclubcorinthianspaulista'\n     >>> normalize_team_name(\"Atlético-MG\")\n-    'atletico'\n+    'atletico|MG'\n     \"\"\"\n     if name is None:\n         return \"\"\n-    s = str(name).strip()\n-    # Remove a trailing \"-XX\" / \" - XX\" two-letter state suffix (case-sensitive\n-    # on the original string so we don't eat legitimate trailing capitals).\n-    s = _TRAILING_STATE_RE.sub(\"\", s)\n-    s = _strip_accents(s)\n-    s = s.lower()\n-    s = re.sub(r\"[^a-z0-9]\", \"\", s)\n-    return s\n+    bare = _bare_key(name)\n+    st = extract_state(name, state)\n+    if st:\n+        return f\"{bare}|{st}\"\n+    return bare\n \n \n def display_team_name(name: str) -> str:\n     \"\"\"Return a human-friendly team name with the state suffix removed.\"\"\"\n     if name is None:\n         return \"\"\n     s = str(name).strip()\n     s = _STATE_SUFFIX_RE.sub(\"\", s)\n-    # also handle \" - XX\" mid/ trailing\n-    s = re.sub(r\"\\s*[-\\u2013]\\s*[A-Z]{2}\\b\", \"\", s)\n     return s.strip()\n \n \n+class TeamResolver:\n+    \"\"\"Resolve raw team names to canonical keys.\n+\n+    Two-phase strategy:\n+    1. Observe every (bare_key, state) pair seen across the datasets.\n+    2. A bare_key that maps to more than one state is *ambiguous* and the\n+       state becomes part of the canonical key (``bare|SP``). A bare_key seen\n+       with at most one state resolves to the bare_key alone, so\n+       ``Palmeiras-SP`` and ``Palmeiras`` collapse to ``palmeiras``.\n+    \"\"\"\n+\n+    def __init__(self) -> None:\n+        self._bare_states: dict[str, set[str]] = {}\n+        self._locked = False\n+\n+    def observe(self, bare_key: str, state: str) -> None:\n+        if self._locked:\n+            return\n+        st = (state or \"\").strip().upper()\n+        if not bare_key:\n+            return\n+        self._bare_states.setdefault(bare_key, set())\n+        if st:\n+            self._bare_states[bare_key].add(st)\n+\n+    def lock(self) -> None:\n+        self._locked = True\n+\n+    def resolve(self, bare_key: str, state: str = \"\") -> str:\n+        \"\"\"Return the canonical key for an observed (bare, state) pair.\"\"\"\n+        if not bare_key:\n+            return \"\"\n+        st = (state or \"\").strip().upper()\n+        states = self._bare_states.get(bare_key, set())\n+        # Ambiguous bare name: state is required to disambiguate.\n+        if len(states) > 1:\n+            return f\"{bare_key}|{st}\" if st else bare_key\n+        # Unambiguous: collapse to bare key (state is redundant).\n+        return bare_key\n+\n+\n # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n # Date parsing\n # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n \n","filediff":{"file":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-e6k_mc30/retort-b02a3ecfac7e/brazilian_soccer_mcp/data_loader.py","patch":"Index: /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-e6k_mc30/retort-b02a3ecfac7e/brazilian_soccer_mcp/data_loader.py\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-e6k_mc30/retort-b02a3ecfac7e/brazilian_soccer_mcp/data_loader.py\n+++ /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-e6k_mc30/retort-b02a3ecfac7e/brazilian_soccer_mcp/data_loader.py\n@@ -42,55 +42,116 @@\n # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n # Canonical team-name normalization\n # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n \n-_STATE_SUFFIX_RE = re.compile(r\"\\s*[-\\u2013]\\s*[A-Z]{2}$\")\n-_TRAILING_STATE_RE = re.compile(r\"\\s*[-\\u2013]\\s*[A-Z]{2}\\b\")\n+# Matches a trailing two-letter Brazilian state suffix, e.g. \"-SP\", \" - MG\".\n+_STATE_SUFFIX_RE = re.compile(r\"\\s*[-\\u2013]\\s*([A-Z]{2})\\s*$\")\n \n \n def _strip_accents(text: str) -> str:\n     \"\"\"Return *text* with combining accents removed (NFKD decomposition).\"\"\"\n     nfkd = unicodedata.normalize(\"NFKD\", text)\n     return \"\".join(ch for ch in nfkd if not unicodedata.combining(ch))\n \n \n-def normalize_team_name(name: str) -> str:\n-    \"\"\"Return a canonical lowercase alphanumeric key for a team name.\n+def _bare_key(name: str) -> str:\n+    \"\"\"Lowercase alphanumeric key with the state suffix removed.\"\"\"\n+    if name is None:\n+        return \"\"\n+    s = str(name).strip()\n+    s = _STATE_SUFFIX_RE.sub(\"\", s)\n+    s = _strip_accents(s)\n+    s = s.lower()\n+    s = re.sub(r\"[^a-z0-9]\", \"\", s)\n+    return s\n \n+\n+def extract_state(name: str, fallback_state: str = \"\") -> str:\n+    \"\"\"Extract a two-letter state suffix from a team name, else return fallback.\"\"\"\n+    if name:\n+        m = _STATE_SUFFIX_RE.search(str(name).strip())\n+        if m:\n+            return m.group(1)\n+    return (fallback_state or \"\").strip().upper()\n+\n+\n+def normalize_team_name(name: str, state: str = \"\") -> str:\n+    \"\"\"Return a canonical lowercase key for a team name.\n+\n+    When *state* is provided (or embedded in *name* as a ``-XX`` suffix) it is\n+    appended to the key so that distinct same-name clubs from different states\n+    (e.g. Atlético-MG vs Atletico-PR) are not merged. When the bare name is\n+    globally unique the state is redundant and the bare key alone is returned\n+    by the :class:`TeamResolver`; this function is the low-level primitive.\n+\n     Examples\n     --------\n     >>> normalize_team_name(\"Palmeiras-SP\")\n+    'palmeiras|SP'\n+    >>> normalize_team_name(\"Palmeiras\")\n     'palmeiras'\n-    >>> normalize_team_name(\"América - MG\")\n-    'america'\n-    >>> normalize_team_name(\"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\")\n-    'sportclubcorinthianspaulista'\n     >>> normalize_team_name(\"Atlético-MG\")\n-    'atletico'\n+    'atletico|MG'\n     \"\"\"\n     if name is None:\n         return \"\"\n-    s = str(name).strip()\n-    # Remove a trailing \"-XX\" / \" - XX\" two-letter state suffix (case-sensitive\n-    # on the original string so we don't eat legitimate trailing capitals).\n-    s = _TRAILING_STATE_RE.sub(\"\", s)\n-    s = _strip_accents(s)\n-    s = s.lower()\n-    s = re.sub(r\"[^a-z0-9]\", \"\", s)\n-    return s\n+    bare = _bare_key(name)\n+    st = extract_state(name, state)\n+    if st:\n+        return f\"{bare}|{st}\"\n+    return bare\n \n \n def display_team_name(name: str) -> str:\n     \"\"\"Return a human-friendly team name with the state suffix removed.\"\"\"\n     if name is None:\n         return \"\"\n     s = str(name).strip()\n     s = _STATE_SUFFIX_RE.sub(\"\", s)\n-    # also handle \" - XX\" mid/ trailing\n-    s = re.sub(r\"\\s*[-\\u2013]\\s*[A-Z]{2}\\b\", \"\", s)\n     return s.strip()\n \n \n+class TeamResolver:\n+    \"\"\"Resolve raw team names to canonical keys.\n+\n+    Two-phase strategy:\n+    1. Observe every (bare_key, state) pair seen across the datasets.\n+    2. A bare_key that maps to more than one state is *ambiguous* and the\n+       state becomes part of the canonical key (``bare|SP``). A bare_key seen\n+       with at most one state resolves to the bare_key alone, so\n+       ``Palmeiras-SP`` and ``Palmeiras`` collapse to ``palmeiras``.\n+    \"\"\"\n+\n+    def __init__(self) -> None:\n+        self._bare_states: dict[str, set[str]] = {}\n+        self._locked = False\n+\n+    def observe(self, bare_key: str, state: str) -> None:\n+        if self._locked:\n+            return\n+        st = (state or \"\").strip().upper()\n+        if not bare_key:\n+            return\n+        self._bare_states.setdefault(bare_key, set())\n+        if st:\n+            self._bare_states[bare_key].add(st)\n+\n+    def lock(self) -> None:\n+        self._locked = True\n+\n+    def resolve(self, bare_key: str, state: str = \"\") -> str:\n+        \"\"\"Return the canonical key for an observed (bare, state) pair.\"\"\"\n+        if not bare_key:\n+            return \"\"\n+        st = (state or \"\").strip().upper()\n+        states = self._bare_states.get(bare_key, set())\n+        # Ambiguous bare name: state is required to disambiguate.\n+        if len(states) > 1:\n+            return f\"{bare_key}|{st}\" if st else bare_key\n+        # Unambiguous: collapse to bare key (state is redundant).\n+        return bare_key\n+\n+\n # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n # Date parsing\n # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n \n","additions":80,"deletions":19},"truncated":false},"title":"brazilian_soccer_mcp/data_loader.py","time":{"start":1788198916028,"end":1788198916031}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The problem: \"Atletico-MG\" and \"Atletico-PR\" both normalize to \"atletico\" because I strip the state suffix. But these are DIFFERENT teams (Atlético Mineiro vs Athletico Paranaense, both written \"Atletico\" in this dataset but distinguished only by state). \n\nThis is a real issue. My normalization is too aggressive — stripping the state suffix loses the disambiguation between same-city-name teams from different states. I need to keep the state in the canonical key when it disambiguates.\n\nThe fix: don't strip the state suffix from the canonical key entirely. Instead, keep a key that includes the state but in normalized form. But then \"Palmeiras-SP\" and \"Palmeiras\" wouldn't match...\n\nThis is the classic team-name normalization problem mentioned in TASK.md. The right approach: build a canonical mapping that resolves known variants. For matching queries, when a user types \"Atletico-MG\" we want to match \"Atletico-MG\" specifically. When they type \"Atletico\" ambiguously... that's ambiguous.\n\nBetter approach: Use a canonical key that PRESERVES the state suffix when present, but ALSO support a fallback \"bare name\" lookup. Actually, the cleanest fix: the canonical key should retain the state. So \"Atletico-MG\" → \"atleticomg\", \"Atletico-PR\" → \"atleticopr\", \"Palmeiras-SP\" → \"palmeirassp\". But then \"Palmeiras\" (no state, from Libertadores) → \"palmeiras\" won't match \"palmeirassp\".\n\nThe real solution is a name-resolution layer: build an alias dictionary mapping every observed (normalized bare name, state) to a canonical team identity. For teams that are unique by bare name, the bare name is the identity. For teams that collide (like Atletico-MG vs Atletico-PR), the state is required to disambiguate.\n\nLet me implement a TeamResolver:\n1. Collect all (bare_name, state) pairs from the data.\n2. For each bare name, if there's only one state (or no state) → canonical key = bare name.\n3. If multiple states for the same bare name → canonical key = bare_name + state.\n\nThen when querying with a user-supplied name:\n- If user provides state (e.g. \"Atletico-MG\") → resolve to the specific team.\n- If user provides bare name that is unique → resolve to that team.\n- If user provides bare name that is ambiguous → we could return all matches for all teams with that bare name, OR pick the most common. For the benchmark, returning all is safer (more inclusive).\n\nActually, simpler and more correct: build the canonical key INCLUDING state when available, but build an ALIAS map so that bare-name lookups resolve to all matching canonical keys.\n\nLet me restructure:\n- canonical_key(team_raw, state) = bare + (\"|\" + state if state and bare is ambiguous else \"\")\n- For indexing: index by canonical_key.\n- For querying: given user input, compute its bare name; find all canonical keys whose bare name matches; if user input included a state, filter to that state.\n\nHmm, this is getting complex. Let me simplify with a resolver that:\n- Maintains `bare -> set of (canonical_key, display_name)` \n- canonical_key = bare + state_suffix when state present and helps disambiguate, else bare.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole normalization. The cleanest robust approach:\n\ncanonical_key = normalize(bare_name_without_state) + (state if state provided else \"\")\n\nSo \"Palmeiras-SP\" → \"palmeiras\"+\"SP\" = \"palmeirasSP\"? But then \"Palmeiras\" (no state) → \"palmeiras\". These won't match.\n\nThe fundamental tension: state suffix sometimes disambiguates (Atletico-MG vs Atletico-PR) and sometimes is just redundant info (Palmeiras-SP). \n\nThe proper solution is an explicit alias/canonical map built from the data. Let me build it:\n\n1. Walk all matches, collect every (raw_name, state) and bare_name.\n2. Group by bare_name. \n3. If a bare_name has multiple distinct states across records → it's ambiguous; canonical identity = bare_name + \"|\" + state. But we need to merge: \"Atletico-MG\" with state \"MG\" and bare \"Atletico\" → identity \"atletico|MG\". Records with bare \"atletico\" but no state (e.g. from Libertadores \"Atletico-MG\" without explicit state column but the raw name contains -MG) → we extract state from raw name.\n4. If bare_name is unique (only one state or no states) → identity = bare_name.\n\nFor querying: user \"Atletico-MG\" → bare \"atletico\", state \"MG\" → identity \"atletico|MG\". User \"Palmeiras\" → bare \"palmeiras\", unique → \"palmeiras\". User \"Atletico\" (ambiguous, no state) → return all teams with bare \"atletico\" (both MG and PR).\n\nThis is the right design. Let me rewrite the normalization to:\n- Extract bare name AND state from a raw name (state = trailing -XX or the separate state column).\n- Build a resolver after loading all data that decides canonical keys.\n\nLet me refactor data_loader.py. I'll:\n1. Add `extract_name_state(raw, state_col)` → (bare_display, bare_key, state).\n2. Build Match with home_team_key computed via resolver.\n3. Add a TeamResolver class built in `_build_indexes` that determines canonical keys.\n\nActually, to keep it simpler, I can compute the canonical key in two passes:\n- Pass 1: collect bare_keys and their states.\n- Pass 2: assign canonical key = bare_key if bare_key unique (single state or no state), else bare_key + state.\n\nLet me restructure. I'll store on each Match the bare_key and state, then after loading compute canonical keys and rebuild team index.\n\nLet me rewrite data_loader.py more carefully.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_058f60fa5001894x5FC905Vb6k","sessionID":"ses_fa710145bffe992PbNwQxTi5bS","messageID":"msg_058f5699c001KJ2Kkr9qtDSeUz"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1788198916035,"sessionID":"ses_fa710145bffe992PbNwQxTi5bS","part":{"id":"prt_058f5c61a001JkP7fvsCZDg2uj","messageID":"msg_058f5699c001KJ2Kkr9qtDSeUz","sessionID":"ses_fa710145bffe992PbNwQxTi5bS","type":"text","text":"Found a real bug: `Atletico-MG` and `Atletico-PR` both collapse to `atletico` because stripping the state suffix merges two distinct clubs. I need a resolver that keeps the state when it disambiguates. Let me rewrite the normalization logic.","time":{"start":1788198897178,"end":1788198916033}}}
