mcp.server import Server\n-from mcp.server.stdio import stdio_server\n+from mcp.server.mcpserver import MCPServer\n from mcp.types import (\n     TextContent,\n     Tool,\n )\n","additions":1,"deletions":2},"truncated":false},"title":"brazilian_soccer_mcp/mcp_server.py","time":{"start":1788199307108,"end":1788199307113}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The lowlevel Server uses `add_request_handler`. This is more manual. The MCPServer high-level is easier. Let me rewrite using MCPServer with `add_tool` wrapper functions. I'll define a wrapper per tool with proper parameter annotations so the schema is inferred, and delegate to dispatch_tool.\n\nActually, to keep full control over descriptions and schemas while using a working API, I'll use MCPServer.add_tool with wrapper functions that have docstrings (used as descriptions) and typed params. Let me write the wrappers.\n\nLet me rewrite mcp_server.py cleanly. I'll keep TOOLS (for the test that checks the list) and dispatch_tool (for tests), and add a `create_server()` that builds an MCPServer with add_tool wrappers.\n\nThe wrappers need signatures. Let me define them with **kwargs and delegate. But add_tool infers schema from the signature; with **kwargs it won't produce a useful schema. Better to define explicit params.\n\nGiven time, let me define wrapper functions with explicit typed parameters for each tool. This is verbose but correct.\n\nActually, a cleaner trick: MCPServer.add_tool accepts `fn` and uses its signature. I can dynamically create functions. But simplest: write them out.\n\nLet me rewrite the server bootstrap section.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_058fc0755001uoYyUuJTyOAomF","sessionID":"ses_fa710145bffe992PbNwQxTi5bS","messageID":"msg_058fbe36f001kdApHSwIZp4sGz"}}
