{"type":"text","timestamp":1788199417493,"sessionID":"ses_fa710145bffe992PbNwQxTi5bS","part":{"id":"prt_058fda02a001NfylHbOTRP1ZWR","messageID":"msg_058fd97e8001qi8de5JOxL6aco","sessionID":"ses_fa710145bffe992PbNwQxTi5bS","type":"text","text":"Done. Everything in `TASK.md` is implemented and verified.\n\n## Summary\n\nBuilt a Brazilian Soccer MCP server in Python with all 6 Kaggle CSVs loaded and queryable.\n\n**Code** (in `brazilian_soccer_mcp/`):\n- `data_loader.py` — loads & normalizes all ~23,954 matches + 18,207 players. A `TeamResolver` strips redundant state suffixes (`Palmeiras-SP` == `Palmeiras`) but **retains** them to disambiguate same-name clubs (`Atletico-MG` ≠ `Atletico-PR`) — a real bug I caught where the 2019 standings merged two distinct clubs. Handles ISO/Brazilian date formats and UTF-8 accents.\n- `queries.py` — `SoccerQueryEngine` covering all 5 capability areas: matches, teams, players, competitions, statistics.\n- `mcp_server.py` — `MCPServer` (stdio JSON-RPC) exposing 15 tools. Verified live: `initialize` handshake + `tools/call` returns `champion: Flamengo` for 2019.\n\n**Tests** — 31 BDD Given/When/Then pytest scenarios across `test_matches/teams/players/competitions/statistics/mcp.py`, all passing in <2s. Covers the spec's success criteria: cross-file queries, name normalization, standings calc, tool discovery/dispatch.\n\n**Verification**: `py_compile` clean, `pytest` → 31 passed, live stdio MCP server responds correctly.\n\nNote: per the repo's git policy I did **not** commit or push — let me know if you'd like me to.","time":{"start":1788199411754,"end":1788199417491}}}
